  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Xie went to the Homosaurus website. I watched xer browse the Homosaurus. Xie uses the Homosaurus xerself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Xe went to the Homosaurus website. I watched xyr browse the Homosaurus. Xe uses the Homosaurus xemself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Xey went to the Homosaurus website. I watched xem browse the Homosaurus. Xey uses the Homosaurus xyr.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Xey went to the Homosaurus website. I watched xem browse the Homosaurus. Xey uses the Homosaurus xeir.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Yo went to the Homosaurus website. I watched yo browse the Homosaurus. Yo uses the Homosaurus yosself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Ze went to the Homosaurus website. I watched zir browse the Homosaurus. Ze uses the Homosaurus zirself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Ze went to the Homosaurus website. I watched hir browse the Homosaurus. Ze uses the Homosaurus hirself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Ze went to the Homosaurus website. I watched zem browse the Homosaurus. Ze uses the Homosaurus zirself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Ze went to the Homosaurus website. I watched mer browse the Homosaurus. Ze uses the Homosaurus zemself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Zee went to the Homosaurus website. I watched zed browse the Homosaurus. Zee uses the Homosaurus zedself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Zie went to the Homosaurus website. I watched zir browse the Homosaurus. Zie uses the Homosaurus zirself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Zie went to the Homosaurus website. I watched zem browse the Homosaurus. Zie uses the Homosaurus zirself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Zie went to the Homosaurus website. I watched hir browse the Homosaurus. Zie uses the Homosaurus hirself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A set of pronouns. Used in this manner: Zme went to the Homosaurus website. I watched zmyr browse the Homosaurus. Zme uses the Homosaurus zmyrself.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Employment of LGBTQ+ individuals.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Employment of lesbians.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Employment of gay men.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Employment of bisexual people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Employment of transgender people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Employment of queer people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbian creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Accounts of LGBTQ+ peoples' lives written by those people. For written accounts of LGBTQ+ people's lives that were not written by the subject, use "LGBTQ+ biographies."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Accounts of lesbians' lives written by those people. For written accounts of lesbians' lives that were not written by the subject, use "Lesbian biographies."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Accounts of gay men's lives written by those people. For written accounts of gay men's lives that were not written by the subject, use "Gay biographies."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Accounts of bisexual peoples' lives written by those people. For written accounts of bisexual people's lives that were not written by the subject, use "Bisexual biographies."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Accounts of trans peoples' lives written by those people. For written accounts of trans people's lives that were not written by the subject, use "Transgender biographies."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Accounts of queer peoples' lives written by those people. For written accounts of queer people's lives that were not written by the subject, use "Queer biographies."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses owned by LGBTQ+ people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses owned by lesbians.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses owned by gay people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses owned by bisexual people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses owned by transgender people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses owned by queer people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses focusing on LGBTQ+ clientele and products related to LGBTQ+ communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses focusing on lesbian clientele and products related to lesbian communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses focusing on gay clientele and products related to gay communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses focusing on bisexual clientele and products related to bisexual communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses focusing on transgender clientele and products related to transgender communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Businesses focusing on queer clientele and products related to queer communities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Asexual creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Aromantic creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit creators of written works.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Written works describing imagined events or people; use in reference to fictional works focusing on Two-Spirit characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Written works of fiction featuring intersex characters or addressing intersex themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Written works describing imagined events or people; use in reference to fictional works focusing on aromantic characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Written works describing imagined events or people; use in reference to fictional works focusing on asexual characters or themes.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A broad grouping term for galleries, libraries, archives, museums, and special collections (GLAMS) that hold materials primarily pertaining to LGBTQ+ people or intended for use by LGBTQ+ people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term used to describe people, typically children, who do not conform to traditional gender norms and instead creatively interweave their internal and external experiences to understand their gender identity and expression. May also be used to describe a way of raising children outside of traditional gender norms.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Families, whether biological, adoptive, or chosen, in which one or more individuals identify as aromantic.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Families, whether biological, adoptive, or chosen, in which one or more individuals identify as asexual.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Families, whether biological, adoptive, or chosen, in which one or more individuals are intersex.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Families, whether biological, adoptive, or chosen, in which one or more individuals identify as Two-Spirit.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Parents who are aromantic.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Parents who are asexual.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Parents who are intersex.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who are parents.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Children who have one or more aromantic parent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Children who have one or more asexual parent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Children who have one or more intersex parent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Children who have one or more Two-Spirit parent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Behavior involving physical force intended to harm or kill Two-Spirit people on the basis of their identity. </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Behavior involving physical force intended to harm or kill intersex people because they are intersex.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchens in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who are trained, professional cooks, typically holding a leadership role in the kitchen in which they are employed.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Slang term for individuals who alternate between being the giving partner and the receiving partner in sexual encounters; typically used as an adjective (e.g., "They're vers.").</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Abbreviated form of the phrase "trans for trans," a label used to describe trans people who prioritize engaging in romantic, sexual, or other relationships with other trans people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Slang term for the act of identifying (or clocking) someone in public as trans, crossdressing, or in drag when that person is intending to pass or be read by others as cisgender, typically for their personal safety. Clocking is often understood to be a threat (of violence or public outing) against the person being clocked. Use the term "Reading (Gender)" to refer to shared identification or recognized kinship between two individuals who are both gender minorities.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Black queer people who recognize their queer identity as salient to their Blackness and vice versa; only use to describe individuals who identify as Blaqueer or BlaQ.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who do not identify as such to others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who do not identify as such to others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who do not identify as such to others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who do not disclose the fact that they are intersex to others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Two-Spirit people who do not identify as such to others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who pose as the date or partner of someone they are not in a relationship with, typically to prevent that person's sexuality from becoming public knowledge; most commonly used to refer to a woman pretending to be dating a gay man.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Abbreviation used to describe Two-Spirit people, lesbians, gay men, bisexual people, transgender people, queer people, and other gender and sexual minorities as a group; commonly used in Canada to emphasize the particularities of Indigenous people's experiences.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Padding or phallic objects designed to be worn in the front of an individual's pants or underwear to give the appearance of having a bulge.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A romantic orientation characterized by the occurrence of romantic attraction or desire in a frequent and consistent manner, often alongside sexual attraction.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A romantic orientation characterized by the occurrence of romantic attraction or desire in a frequent and consistent manner toward more than one sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A romantic orientation characterized by the occurrence of romantic attraction or desire in a frequent and consistent manner toward members of the same sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A romantic orientation characterized by the occurrence of romantic attraction or romantic desire in a frequent and consistent manner toward members of the opposite sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A romantic orientation characterized by the occurrence of romantic attraction or romantic desire in a frequent and consistent manner irrespective of sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who belong to two or more racial groups.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who belong to two or more racial groups.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who belong to two or more racial groups.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who belong to two or more racial groups.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who belong to two or more racial groups.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Sexual attraction or erotic desire between members of the same sex or gender; typically used to describe temporary, latent, or unfulfilled desire rather than a more permanent sexual identity or orientation.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Statements used to caution individuals that materials may be disturbing or offensive. Includes warnings assigned to fanworks (usually by the creator) that give information about possibly upsetting, triggering, or controversial elements within a work.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Sets of clothing worn to emulate a particular person, place, thing, or time period.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Indigenous queer people who recognize their queer identity as salient to their Indigenous identity and vice versa; some but not all Indigiqueer people also identify as Two-Spirit; only use to describe individuals who self-identify as Indigiqueer.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term used to describe the identity of people who are both Indigenous and queer and view those identities as inherently related and interconnected.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A group of Two-Spirit people united by their shared identity.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Special collections that hold materials primarily pertaining to LGBTQ+ people or intended for use by LGBTQ+ people. Special collections tend to be a blend of institutional types (e.g., an archives with a museum component). This term should be applied to institutions that refer to themselves as special collections; otherwise, use the term "LGBTQ+ cultural heritage institutions."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who express fetishistic interest in and pursue sexual encounters with others based solely on their affiliation with a (typically marginalized) group that the chaser does not belong to. Often coupled with other terms to identify the type of chaser.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have moved to another country.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Behaviors, acts, or forms of expression that contradict or do not conform to a given culture's norms surrounding gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals whose gender identities and practices differ from those of the dominant culture. Use the term "Sexual minorities" for individuals whose sexual identities, orientations, and practices differ from those of the dominant culture.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The practice or policy of providing equal access and support to trans people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The practice or policy of denying equal access and support to trans people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Masculine-aligned identities or practices of trans people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Feminine-aligned identities or practices of trans people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A term originating from Greek mythology that refers to beings that are both fully female and fully male. The term was once adopted in the medical realm to describe people with intersex variations generally (and later to describe someone with both ovarian and testicular tissue specifically), but it is now recognized to be stigmatizing, imprecise, and misleading. Many people in the intersex community consider the use of this term to be pathologizing and deeply offensive, while other intersex people have chosen to reclaim the term to describe themselves and their bodies. Do not use this term to describe an individual unless they explicitly identify with it and have chosen to use it.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who experience romantic attraction or desire in a frequent and consistent manner, often alongside sexual attraction.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who experience romantic attraction or desire in a frequent and consistent manner toward people of more than one sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who experience romantic attraction or desire in a frequent and consistent manner toward members of the same sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who experience romantic attraction or romantic desire in a frequent and consistent manner toward members of the opposite sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who experience romantic attraction or romantic desire toward others in a frequent and consistent manner irrespective of sex or gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The direction or nature of an individual's romantic attraction toward others.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals whose romantic orientations and/or practices differ from those of the dominant culture.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Publications or serials by or about LGBTQ+ people; use a more specific term if possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Cultural experiences shared by members of LGBTQ+ communities or products of LGBTQ+ culture (e.g., art and media created by and for LGBTQ+ culture, activism focused on LGBTQ+ issues, slang commonly used in LGBTQ+ communities, etc.). Use a more specific term if possible.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Cultural experiences shared by members of queer communities or products of queer culture (e.g., art and media created by and for queer people, activism focused on queer issues, slang commonly used in queer communities, etc.).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Use to describe cultural experiences shared by members of bisexual communities or products of bisexual culture (e.g., art and media created by and for bisexual people, activism focused on bisexual issues, slang commonly used in bisexual communities, etc.).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Ceasing or reversing the process of gender transition for any reason, including personal safety, stigma, costs associated with transitioning, health concerns, to regain fertility, etc.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Restarting the process of gender transition after a period of detransitioning.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">No longer identifying as transgender, or, more broadly, no longer identifying as some particular gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any of various surgeries that involve modifying the face or facial features to affirm the patient's gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any of various surgeries undertaken to masculinize the face or facial features.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any of various surgeries undertaken to feminize the face or facial features.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Colloquial term for gender-affirming surgical alterations of the chest.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Reconstruction of the chest, typically following a mastectomy, to give the chest a more masculine appearance.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any of various surgical procedures undertaken to feminize the voice.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Any of various surgical procedures undertaken to masculinize the voice.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical construction of the clitoris, often undertaken as a form of gender affirming surgery.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical construction of the labia.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical procedure in which the urethra is elongated and placed along a neophallus, typically to allow for standing urination.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical construction of the scrotum.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical removal of the vagina.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A surgery in which the laryngeal prominence, colloquially referred to as the Adam's apple, is reduced in size, often as part of facial feminization surgery for transfeminine people. Cisgender men and cisgender women may also undergo tracheal shaves if they feel their laryngeal prominence is too prominent.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical construction of the penis.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Surgical construction of the vagina.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Devices designed to allow individuals to pee while standing up; often used by trans men or transmasculine people to pee in public urinals without risking their safety.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have left their home countries and are seeking protection from persecution and/or human rights violations in other countries but have not yet been legally recognized as refugees.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who are disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who are disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who are disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who are disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who are disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who are disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people with significant or total hearing loss who may or may not consider themselves d/Deaf.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians with significant or total hearing loss who may or may not consider themselves d/Deaf.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men with significant or total hearing loss who may or may not consider themselves d/Deaf.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people with significant or total hearing loss who may or may not consider themselves d/Deaf.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people with significant or total hearing loss who may or may not consider themselves d/Deaf.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people with significant or total hearing loss who may or may not consider themselves d/Deaf.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians with significant or total loss of vision.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men with significant or total loss of vision.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people with significant or total loss of vision.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people with significant or total loss of vision.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people with significant or total loss of vision.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who use wheelchairs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The community of Deaf LGBTQ+ people who share a cultural and/or linguistic identity.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The unjust or prejudicial treatment of Two-Spirit people due to their identity; may include the denial of legal rights, employment, and housing.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who use wheelchairs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who use wheelchairs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who use wheelchairs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who use wheelchairs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who use wheelchairs.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A shortened form of the word "cripple" that has been reclaimed by disabled people to express pride and identify their political and community ties. Only use in reference to individuals or organizations that self-identify as crip. Because of the many intersections between crip theory/culture and queer theory/culture, the two are often but not always viewed as related.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The intersection of transphobia and misogyny as directed toward trans women and transfeminine people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The unjust or prejudicial treatment of intersex people due to the fact that they are intersex; may include the denial of legal rights, employment, and housing.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A surgically constructed penis; typically made using tissue grafts from other parts of the body.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term used in the 18th and 19th centuries to describe the act of dressing in disguise, often to emulate another gender. Only use in historical contexts.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The state of individuals born with the sexual anatomy, reproductive system, and chromosomes associated with their assigned sex (i.e., not intersex).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals born with the sexual anatomy, reproductive system, and chromosomes associated with their assigned sex (i.e., people who are not intersex).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Masculine identity associated with Black lesbian communities in the United States.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gender expression that does not conform to a single, distinct gender identity.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Slang term used in drag and ballroom communities to describe the act of playfully or wittily insulting someone's appearance or behavior; described by drag queen Dorian Corey as "the art form of the insult." </subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The ability of a person who is not heterosexual to "pass," or be accepted or regarded as heterosexual by others. "Passing" has historically been used to describe an individual's ability to "pass" as a certain race or ethnicity, though the term was later appropriated by the LGBTQ+ community.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A multidiscplinary field of study and intellectual framework used by scholars and activists to examine the intersections of race, class, and law in the United States, as well as to challenge mainstream U.S. liberal approaches to racial justice. Key concepts of CRT include the argument that race is a social construct with no biological basis and that racism and disparate racial outcomes are the result of complex, changing, and often subtle social and institutional dynamics, rather than individuals' explicit and intentional prejudices.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term coined by coined by Trudy of the womanist blog Gradient Lair to describe the intersection of transphobia, misogyny, and anti-Black racism as directed toward Black trans women.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term coined by scholar Moya Bailey to describe the intersection of misogyny and anti-Black racism as directed toward Black women.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Popularized by the gay liberation group Gays Activists Alliance, zaps are public demonstrations focused on confronting or embarrassing specific politicians or public officials while calling attention to the liberation movement.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Performers who dress and/or use makeup to produce, emphasize, and/or exaggerate traits associated with a certain gender, which may or may not be the gender the performer primarily identifies with.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ practitioners of Wicca or other Pagan religions who self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who practice Wicca or other Pagan religions and self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who practice Wicca or other Pagan religions and self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual practitioners of Wicca or other Pagan religions who self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender practitioners of Wicca or other Pagan religions who self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer practitioners of Wicca or other Pagan religions who self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex practitioners of Wicca or other Pagan religions who self-identify as witches.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Identity term used by Indigenous people in North America to reconnect with the multiple gender and sexual roles in Indigenous cultures.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who are attracted to only one gender; includes gay people and straight people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who engage in sexual or romantic relationships with only one person at a time.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who generally experience sexual attraction or the desire for partnered sexual experiences.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Identity term used to describe the intersection of trans identity and genderqueer identity.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Individuals who experience sexual attraction to more than one gender; includes bisexual and pansexual people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gender identity that exists outside of the gender binary; may be used as a specific gender identity or as an umbrella term in the Euro-American gender system for gender identities outside of the gender binary.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gender identity that does not conform to the gender binary and emphasizes alignment with queer politics.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gender identity that does not conform to the gender binary, not to be confused with gender non-conforming practices engaged in by cisgender people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Umbrella term for gender identities that emphasize a partial connection to a certain gender or concept of gender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term used to describe people who identify with the sex they were assigned at birth.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Term used to describe the identity of people who identify with the sex or gender they were assigned at birth.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Sexual identity used to describe sexual and/or romantic attraction to people of all genders.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Identity term used to describe people who do not have genders or who identify with genderlessness.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Acronym primarily used in the United States to describe Black, Indigenous, and people of color as a group, while also centering the specific experiences of Black and Indigenous people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or amateur sports players who self-identify as LGBTQ+.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or amateur/recreational sports players who self-identify as lesbians.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or amateur/recreational sports players who self-identify as gay men.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or amateur/recreational sports players who are bisexual.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or recreational sports players who self-identify as transgender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or recreational sports players who self-identify as queer.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or amateur sports players who are intersex.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Professional or recreational sports players who self-identify as Two-Spirit.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">LGBTQ+ people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Lesbians who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Gay men who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bisexual people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Transgender people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli and transgender.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Queer people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="680" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Intersex people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai&#x2018;i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.</subfield>
